# Model delegation and cost control

The parent premium model primarily handles architecture, planning, coordination,
escalation, and final review. Keep ambiguous diagnosis, architectural choices, and
unresolved design decisions with the parent. Proactively use `implementer` for 
well-understood, bounded implementation after the approach is settled; task size
alone is not a reason to delegate. For repetitive, boilerplate, straightforward 
work, use `mechanical`.  After substantial, architectural, or security-sensitive 
changes, use `reviewer` for an independent read-only review; the parent evaluates 
findings before fixes.

Keep trivial edits local when delegation overhead exceeds the work. Give each agent
a small, bounded task, relevant paths and context, concrete existing examples to
follow, acceptance criteria, behaviors to preserve, and required checks. For bug
fixes, require evidence of the cause before edits, rather than speculative patches.
Avoid overlapping file ownership, duplicate exploration, recursive delegation, and
unbounded retries. Stop and escalate after two unsuccessful fix attempts on the
same issue, or immediately when architecture or requirements are unclear. The
parent reassesses the evidence and approach before assigning more work.
The parent remains accountable for integration, verification, and the final result.

Honor user instructions and project-local constraints over these defaults. Agents
must follow the same security, required-reading, protected-file, and consent rules.
Delegation never grants permission to commit, push, deploy, or expand scope.

Use the named agents' configured models. If an agent/model is unavailable or Cursor
reports fallback, surface the limitation; do not silently substitute premium work
at scale. Model selection is a preference, not a guaranteed spending cap. Keep
prompts and result summaries focused and verify actual routing in Cursor.

